# the argument of mill-proof.md as claude-opus-5-5 read it: check that it says what the text says
# Mill's proof of utility. S1 and S2 (visible/audible) are analogies offered for S3 and are left out.
# S3's principle is rendered as applied to happiness: that people desire it is the evidence that it is desirable (a good).
atom	desired_own	each person, so far as he believes it to be attainable, desires his own happiness
atom	own_good	each person's happiness is a good to that person
atom	general_good	the general happiness is a good to the aggregate of all persons
atom	happiness_good	happiness is a good
atom	end_of_conduct	happiness is one of the ends of conduct
atom	criterion	happiness is one of the criteria of morality
P1	S3	desired_own → own_good
P2	S5	general_good → desired_own
P3	S6	desired_own	kind empirical
C1	S6	own_good	from P1 P3
# "and the general happiness, therefore, a good to the aggregate": the move from each person's good to the general good is relied on but not stated.
A1	-	own_good → general_good
C2	S6	general_good	from C1 A1
A2	-	own_good ∧ general_good → happiness_good
C3	S6	happiness_good	from C1 C2 A2
A3	-	happiness_good → end_of_conduct
# "and consequently one of the criteria of morality"
A4	-	end_of_conduct → criterion
C	S7	end_of_conduct ∧ criterion	from C3 A3 A4
